      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We want to find four consecutive integers whose product is 840. To solve the problem, we first find the prime factorization of 840.\n\n\\[ 840 = 2^3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\]\n\nSince we are looking for four consecutive integers, their product should also be divisible by \\(2^4\\), \\(3\\), \\(5^2\\), and \\(7\\). Hence, their product should be divisible by \\(2^4 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7^2 = 8^4\\).\n\nSince 840 is not a power of 8, the integers we are looking for must include a 2 to make up for the discrepancy. So let's denote the four consecutive integers as \\(n, n+1, n+2, n+3\\). The only way to achieve the factorization above is if one of the integers is 8, since \\(8^4 = 64 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7^2\\).\n\nAfter determining that one of the integers is 8, the other three integers must be (8-1), (8+1), and (8+2) - all of which must also be consecutive:\n\n- (8 - 1) = 7\n- (8 + 1) = 9\n- (8 + 2) = 10\n\nHence, the four consecutive integers beginning with 8, are 8, 9, 10, and 11.\n\nThe largest of the four integers is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
